<H1 class=3D"node-title">Hiroshima and the power of pictures</H1>
<DIV class=3D"node-author"><SPAN class=3D"even">Hugh =
Gusterson</SPAN></DIV>
<DIV class=3D"author-box">
<DIV class=3D"view view-authors view-id-authors =
view-display-id-panel_pane_1 =
view-dom-id-f95da1185d1b6937c2f42ee3a31d4e1d">
<DIV class=3D"view-content">
<DIV class=3D"views-row views-row-1 views-row-odd views-row-first =
views-row-last">
<DIV class=3D"views-field views-field-field-bio-media-photo">
<DIV class=3D"field-content"><A =
href=3D"http://thebulletin.org/bio/hugh-gusterson">
<DIV class=3D"file file-image file-image-jpeg" id=3D"file-278">
<H2 class=3D"element-invisible">gusterson.jpeg</H2>
<DIV class=3D"content"><IMG title=3D"Hugh Gusterson" alt=3D"Hugh =
Gusterson" =
src=3D"http://thebulletin.org/sites/default/files/styles/scale_width_90px=
/public/gusterson.jpeg?itok=3D9ELw6VpU"=20
typeof=3D"foaf:Image"> </DIV></DIV></A></DIV></DIV>
<H3 class=3D"author-title"><A=20
href=3D"http://thebulletin.org/bio/hugh-gusterson">Hugh =
Gusterson</A></H3>
<DIV class=3D"teaser-text">
<P>Hugh Gusterson is a professor of anthropology and international =
affairs at=20
George Washington University. His expertise is in nuclear culture, =
international=20
security, and the anthropology of...</P><A class=3D"views-more-link" =
href=3D"http://thebulletin.org/bio/hugh-gusterson">More</A></DIV>
<DIV class=3D"contact"></DIV>
<DIV class=3D"feed"><A=20
href=3D"http://thebulletin.org/bio/144/feed">Subscribe</A></DIV>
<DIV class=3D"twitter"></DIV>
<DIV class=3D"facebook"></DIV>
<DIV class=3D"linkedin"></DIV></DIV></DIV></DIV></DIV>
<DIV class=3D"body-text">
<P>Sixty-four years ago this week the Japanese cities of Hiroshima and =
Nagasaki=20
were destroyed by atomic bombs. Whether we endorse or condemn the =
bombings, how=20
do we grasp the enormity of the destruction that befell those two =
unfortunate=20
Japanese cities? The last survivors of the bombings are passing into =
history,=20
taking with them the power of their living witness. But for me, the full =
force=20
of the bombings has always come from pictures more than words.</P>
<P>There is, of course, the <A =
href=3D"http://nuclearweaponarchive.org/Japan/Hirosh1.gif"=20
target=3D"_blank">iconic image</A> of the mushroom cloud rising above =
Hiroshima,=20
and the <A =
href=3D"http://www.smh.com.au/ffximage/2004/12/31/hiroshima_wideweb__430x=
323.jpg"=20
target=3D"_blank">famous aerial picture</A> of an almost entirely =
flattened city.=20
But both pictures have a distanced and abstract quality, bereft as they =
are of=20
people. As aerial shots, both pictures also embody the point of view of =
those=20
who dropped the Bomb more than those who experienced its destructive =
power=20
close-up.</P>
<BLOCKQUOTE>
  <P>The na=C3=AFvet=C3=A9 about the physical effects of nuclear weapons =
after the=20
  bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki deformed public debates about =
nuclear=20
  weapons policy in the years after World War II just as our ignorance =
today=20
  about the full range of detainee abuse in Iraq is inhibiting a fully =
candid=20
  and informed debate about that war."</P></BLOCKQUOTE>
<P>To grasp the victims' experience, you have to move to the ground and =
zoom in.=20
Images like this <A =
href=3D"http://4.bp.blogspot.com/_PgfRBZetNGE/SJmvmAcg6dI/AAAAAAAAAI8/H3R=
fT1McG9Q/s400/hiroshima-portrait-100days-ga.jpg"=20
target=3D"_blank">incongruously formal portrait</A> of mother and child =
use urban=20
destruction as a backdrop to evoke the existential isolation of =
survivors=20
stranded in a ruined landscape. This image also reminds us that, with =
most adult=20
men fighting at the front, the majority of victims in Hiroshima and =
Nagasaki=20
were women and children.</P>
<P>Harder to look at, pictures like <A =
href=3D"http://www.tamilnation.org/images/humanrights/hiroshima1.gif"=20
target=3D"_blank">this</A> show what the Bomb did to human bodies.</P>
<P>In her book <EM>Regarding the Pain of Others</EM>, Susan Sontag =
describes the=20
best war pictures as "the visual equivalent of sound bites," as they =
have the=20
ability to condense complex issues with great persuasive force. Sontag =
points=20
out that pictures of the victims of violence don't always evoke =
sympathy.=20
Southern whites in an earlier age, for example, often reacted to =
pictures of=20
black lynching victims with glee, keeping them as visual trophies. But =
pictures=20
of war victims, especially if they were noncombatants or were hurt in =
ways that=20
violate the norms of war, also have tremendous power to incite shame, =
disgust,=20
anger and conflict in a way that mere verbal accounts do not. Sontag =
points to=20
the importance of two famous pictures from Vietnam in crystallizing =
American=20
disillusion with that war: One is Huynh Cong Ut's <A =
href=3D"http://ducle.files.wordpress.com/2008/05/nick-ut-kim-phuc-vietnam=
-war.jpg"=20
target=3D"_blank">picture</A> of a naked Vietnamese girl running in =
agony from a=20
napalm attack, and the other is Eddie Adams's <A =
href=3D"http://pulitzerphotos.files.wordpress.com/2008/09/century0258.jpe=
g"=20
target=3D"_blank">picture</A> of a Vietcong suspect, handcuffed, being =
executed on=20
the street by the South Vietnamese police.</P>
<P>It is the potentially incendiary quality of war pictures that led =
U.S.=20
occupying authorities to censor pictures from Hiroshima and Nagasaki =
after the=20
war. The Australian journalist Wilfred Burchett had his pictures of =
Hiroshima=20
confiscated and was then expelled from Japan. The Japanese =
photojournalist=20
Yoshito Matsushige survived the atomic bombing and took the only =
photographs of=20
Hiroshima survivors that day, only to have them confiscated until 1952. =
Akira=20
Iwasaki filmed the aftermath of the bombings, but his footage was seized =
and=20
taken to the United States, not to be returned to Japan until 1968. For =
many=20
years the sole images of the bombings in Japan were watercolor paintings =
by=20
survivors. Even these could have great power, as demonstrated in a class =
from my=20
days teaching at MIT when a guest lecture on the paintings by John =
Dower, a=20
historian of Japan, caused one student to faint. (For more on the =
censorship of=20
photos of the aftereffects of the Hiroshima and Nagasaki bombings, see =
<A =
href=3D"http://observatory.designobserver.com/entry.html?entry=3D7517"=20
target=3D"_blank">"Hiroshima: The Lost Photographs."</A>)</P>
<P>Fifty years after the fact, it became evident that the original =
photographs=20
of Hiroshima still retained their incendiary power. Even after they had=20
succeeded in neutering most of the commentary by professional historians =
for the=20
Smithsonian Museum's planned exhibit on Hiroshima and Nagasaki, veterans =
groups=20
and right-wing pundits were incensed that the museum was still planning =
to=20
exhibit photographs from Hiroshima, as well as a Japanese schoolgirl's =
charred=20
lunchbox. Rather than allow this, they insisted the exhibit be shrunken =
down to=20
this: the Enola Gay, the plane that dropped the first bomb, and a modest =
plaque=20
about what it did.</P>
<P>The sixty-fourth anniversary of Hiroshima and Nagasaki is a good time =
to=20
reflect on these issues since this is the year that President Barack =
Obama,=20
following in General Douglas MacArthur's footsteps, decided that it =
would serve=20
no purpose to make public another round of images of U.S. soldiers =
abusing=20
detainees in Iraq. Some of the images reportedly show rape of prisoners, =
sexual=20
abuse of minors, and the frank physical violence with which some =
detainees have=20
been treated.</P>
<P>President Obama is doubtless correct that the public circulation of =
such=20
pictures would incite a further wave of revulsion against the U.S. =
occupation of=20
Iraq, possibly endangering American lives, just as General MacArthur was =
surely=20
correct that the publication of pictures from Hiroshima would have =
incited=20
criticism of the United States and nuclear weapons. But historians have =
noted=20
the costs of MacArthur's censorship regime: the pall of silence about =
the Bomb=20
that it took Japan decades to shake off; the inability of survivors to =
get=20
medical information about their condition, given the censorship of =
medical=20
journal articles on Hiroshima and Nagasaki; and the U.S. public's =
ignorance=20
about what was done in their name in Japan in August 1945. The resulting =
na=C3=AFvet=C3=A9=20
about the physical effects of nuclear weapons deformed public debates =
about=20
nuclear weapons policy in the years after World War II just as our =
ignorance=20
today about the full range of detainee abuse in Iraq is inhibiting a =
fully=20
candid and informed debate about that war.</P>
<P>If past is prologue, we will, one day, see the pictures Obama has =
kept from=20
us, just as we eventually came to see the grainy black-and-white images =
of=20
unspeakable suffering from Hiroshima and Nagasaki. He can delay--but he =
cannot=20
prevent--the materialization of this visual truth. Maybe there is in =
that cache=20
an image we haven't yet seen that has the power to become as iconic as =
the=20
napalmed girl in Vietnam, to become the visual soundbite that =
encapsulates the=20
U.S. occupation of Iraq.</P>
<P>Many years ago I was talking with a retired nuclear weapons designer =
in his=20
living room when he went to his study to get <A =
href=3D"http://www.tamilnation.org/images/humanrights/hiroshima9.jpg"=20
target=3D"_blank">this picture</A> of a Hiroshima survivor to show me. =
When I=20
looked, I instinctively winced, imagining the pain of those burns and =
thinking=20
of countless more people with burns like that if nuclear weapons were =
used=20
again. But he saw something different in the picture. He talked =
dispassionately=20
about the pattern of the burns. The woman had been wearing a =
black-and-white=20
blouse and hadn't been burned at all where the blouse was white and =
reflected=20
the heat. The picture for him was evidence that simple precautions could =
protect=20
people if nuclear weapons were used again.</P>
<P>We looked at the same picture and saw different things, but the =
picture gave=20
us something to talk about. That's what pictures do. They evoke =
feelings. They=20
convey information. They provoke different responses. They incite =
conversations.=20
And they allow us to feel as if we were there. That's why museums and =
history=20
books need to keep showing pictures of what was done in our name in =
Hiroshima.=20
And that's why, before too long, we should see more pictures of what was =
done in=20
our name in Iraq.</P></DIV></DIV>
